Follow the [[g:test pyramid]]:
- lots of [[b:unit tests]] (JUnit 5 + Mockito, pure domain logic, milliseconds)
- fewer: [[b:integration tests]]
- on top: a handful of [[b:e2e tests]]

- Spring [[g:slice tests]] (`@WebMvcTest`, `@DataJpaTest`) load only part of the context → fast
- test real infrastructure (DB, Kafka) with [[g:Testcontainers]] (Docker) instead of H2, to stay close to production
- [[g:contract tests]] (Spring Cloud Contract/Pact) keep provider–consumer compatibility from breaking when APIs change

:::deep
[[r:Anti-pattern]]: cramming every test into `@SpringBootTest` (loads the whole context &#8594; slow, brittle) instead of using slice/unit tests.

**[[r:H2 lies]]**: its dialect/locking/data types differ from MySQL/Postgres &#8594; tests pass green but production fails; Testcontainers runs the real engine inside Docker.

- [[r:Flaky tests]] are usually caused by time/order/thread dependencies → replace `Thread.sleep` with Awaitility, isolate data per test
- fintech/OTA: test *idempotency* (calling twice with the same key &#8594; one effect) + *concurrency* (two threads debiting money/booking a seat at once &#8594; correct final balance)
- *property-based testing* (jqwik) for money calculations (BigDecimal, rounding); measure quality with *mutation testing* (PIT), not just coverage %

**Runnable integration boundary — let the real PostgreSQL constraint answer:**
<pre><code>@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class BookingDbIT {
  @Container @ServiceConnection
  static PostgreSQLContainer postgres =
      new PostgreSQLContainer("postgres:18-alpine");
  @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;
  @Test void duplicateReferenceIsRejected() {
    jdbc.execute("create table booking(reference text primary key)");
    assertThat(jdbc.update("insert into booking values (?)", "B-42")).isEqualTo(1);
    assertThatThrownBy(() -&gt;
        jdbc.update("insert into booking values (?)", "B-42"))
        .isInstanceOf(DataIntegrityViolationException.class);
  }
}
# test deps: spring-boot-testcontainers, testcontainers-postgresql
./mvnw -Dtest=BookingDbIT test</code></pre>

`@ServiceConnection` supplies connection details to Boot; the test does not copy a random mapped port into properties. The container is the Testcontainers 2.x type, which is no longer generic (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q1).

- **Benefit:** it exercises real DDL, driver error translation and database semantics.
- **Cost:** Docker startup and schema setup are slower, so keep fast domain tests and reserve this layer for boundaries where an in-memory substitute could lie.

**Boundary matrix — each layer proves only part:**
<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Test</th><th>It does not prove</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Domain</td><td>unit/property tests for rules and money</td><td>proxy, driver, database or serialization behavior</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spring proxy/context</td><td>proxy, self-invocation, commit/rollback and async/reactive-context integration tests</td><td>real database locking or broker delivery</td></tr>
<tr><td>Web/JPA slice</td><td>controller binding, validation, repository mapping and error shape</td><td>full auto-config, network, failover or production topology</td></tr>
<tr><td>Real infrastructure</td><td>Testcontainers with the target database/driver and failure injection</td><td>all replicas, rollout surge or every provider version</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public contract</td><td>JSON snapshots, compatibility and provider-consumer contract tests</td><td>authorization correctness unless fields and principals are asserted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operational failure</td><td>timeout, cancellation, pool exhaustion, crash-after-commit and shutdown tests</td><td>recovery in an untested deployment configuration</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

Use this boundary map with the integration and failure-test guidance in (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q2).
